<title>UNITED NATIONS: First states to sign the nuclear treaty.</title>
<headline>First states to sign the nuclear treaty.</headline>
<dateline>UNITED NATIONS 1996-09-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>The first 15 countries to follow President Bill Clinton in signing the Comprehensive Test Ban Treaty after the nuclear pact was open for signature early on Tuesday were:</p>
<p>China, France, Russia, Britain, Japan, the Netherlands, Germany, Denmark, Austria, Italy, Canada, Chile, Australia, Ireland and South Africa.</p>
<p>In the afternoon, countries scheduled to sign were: Belgium, Micronesia, Mexico, Norway, Finland, Turkey, Sweden, Romania, Iceland, Latvia, Cyprus, Poland, Belarus, Indonesia, Greece, Slovenia, Switzerland, Marshall Islands, Iran, Bosnia, Brazil, Costa Rica, Spain, Luxembourg, Portugal, Turkmenistan, Bulgaria, South Korea, Burundi, Seychelles, Georgia, Uruguay, Argentina, Sudan, Nicaragua, Phillippines, Haiti, Exuador, Croatia, Colombia, Kuwait, Panama and Bolivia.</p>
<p>Among the countries which have given notice they would sign over the next two weeks are: Israel, Hungary, Sao Tome and Principe, Senegal, New Zealand, Liechtenstein, Ukraine, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Monaco, Armenia, Solomon Islands, Gabon, Lithuania, Kyrgyztan and Somoa.</p>
</text>
